# Recipe: an invoice

A logo, the parties, line items with VAT, totals, page numbers, archived as PDF/A-3b. The example in
the gem: `stationery examples invoice --source` (and `stationery examples e_invoice --source` for
the Factur-X e-invoice built on it).

```ruby
class InvoicePdf < Stationery::Document
  LOGO = File.expand_path("logo.png", __dir__)        # in Rails: Rails.root.join("app/assets/images/logo.png").to_s
  ACCENT = "#4F46E5"
  MUTED = "#6B7280"

  page size: :a4, margin: [40, 44, 56, 44]
  default_text size: 9, color: "#1F2937"
  metadata title: "Invoice", author: "Acme Studio AB", lang: "en"
  conformance :pdf_a3b, missing_glyphs: :replace      # PDF/A-3b; a character no font has is drawn as a stand-in

  footer do |page|                                    # the seller's details, as invoices carry them
    row do
      column(width: 0.8) { text "Acme Studio AB · Storgatan 1, 111 22 Stockholm · VAT SE556677889901", size: 7, color: MUTED }
      column { text "Page #{page.number} of #{page.count}", size: 7, color: MUTED, align: :right }
    end
  end

  Line = Struct.new(:description, :quantity, :unit_cents, :vat_rate)

  def self.preview
    new(number: "INV-2026-042", date: "29 September 2026", due: "29 October 2026",
        customer: ["Müller & Söhne GmbH", "Hauptstraße 5", "10115 Berlin", "Germany"],
        lines: [Line.new("Brand workshop", 1, 240_000, 25), Line.new("Illustration set", 12, 18_000, 25),
                Line.new("Printed guide", 3, 4_500, 6)])
  end

  def initialize(number:, date:, due:, customer:, lines:)
    super()
    @number = number
    @date = date
    @due = due
    @customer = customer
    @lines = lines
  end

  def view_template
    row(align: :middle) do
      column(width: 0.6) { text "Invoice #{@number}", size: 22, weight: :bold }
      column { image LOGO, height: 34, align: :right }
    end
    spacer 10                                           # else the logo sits on the rule
    rule height: 2, color: ACCENT
    spacer 18
    row(gap: 24) do
      column(width: 0.5) { party("BILL TO", @customer) }
      column do
        table([["Invoice date", @date], ["Due date", @due]], widths: [80, nil],
              cell: { borders: [], padding: [1, 0, 2, 0] }) { |t| t.column(0).weight = :bold }
      end
    end
    spacer 24
    line_items
    spacer 20
    text "Pay to IBAN SE45 5000 0000 0583 9825 7466 within 30 days.", color: MUTED, keep_with_next: true
  end

  private

  def party(label, lines)
    text label, size: 7.5, weight: :bold, color: MUTED, letter_spacing: 0.5
    spacer 3
    text lines.join("\n"), leading: 1                   # a plain String: "&" and "<" print as they are
  end

  def line_items
    rows = [["Description", "Qty", "Unit price", "VAT", "Amount"]]
    @lines.each do |line|
      rows << [line.description, line.quantity.to_s, money(line.unit_cents), "#{line.vat_rate}%",
               money(line.quantity * line.unit_cents)]
    end
    rows << [{ content: "Subtotal", colspan: 4 }, money(subtotal)]
    vat_by_rate.each { |rate, cents| rows << [{ content: "VAT #{rate}%", colspan: 4 }, money(cents)] }
    rows << [{ content: "Total due", colspan: 4 }, money(subtotal + vat_by_rate.values.sum)]
    totals = rows.size - @lines.size - 1

    table(rows, width: :full, widths: [nil, 40, 75, 45, 80], header: true,
                cell: { borders: [], padding: [6, 8] }) do |t|
      t.row(0).set(background: ACCENT, color: "#FFFFFF", weight: :bold)
      t.columns(1..).align = :right
      t.zebra(from: 2, to: @lines.size, color: "#F9FAFB")
      t.rows(-totals..).align = :right
      t.row(-totals).set(borders: [:top], border_color: "#E5E7EB", border_width: 1)
      t.row(-1).set(weight: :bold, size: 11)
    end
  end

  def subtotal = @lines.sum { |line| line.quantity * line.unit_cents }

  def vat_by_rate
    @lines.group_by(&:vat_rate).transform_values do |lines|
      (lines.sum { |line| line.quantity * line.unit_cents } * lines.first.vat_rate / 100.0).round
    end
  end

  def money(cents)
    whole, fraction = cents.abs.divmod(100)
    "#{"-" if cents.negative?}€#{whole.to_s.reverse.scan(/\d{1,3}/).join(",").reverse}.#{format("%02d", fraction)}"
  end
end
```

## What matters here

- **Money is computed by the application**, in integer cents or `BigDecimal`, never in Floats;
  the document only formats it. Totals are ordinary table rows: a cell `{ content:, colspan: }`
  spans columns, `t.rows(-3..)` selects the last three rows.
- **A table has black 0.5 pt borders on every cell by default.** `cell: { borders: [] }` takes
  them away; `borders: [:top]` on a selection draws one rule. `header: true` repeats the first row
  on every page the table continues onto; `t.zebra(from:, to:, color:)` stripes rows.
- **`widths:`** gives columns in points (`nil` shares what is left); `width: :full` makes the table
  as wide as the page.
- **PDF/A-3b** (`conformance :pdf_a3b`) embeds the sRGB output intent and XMP identification, and
  raises `Stationery::ConformanceError` instead of writing a file that breaks the claim: a CMYK
  colour, `encrypt`, or a character no font has (unless `missing_glyphs: :replace`, as above). Set
  `metadata title:`. Transparency (a PNG logo with alpha) is allowed. Validate the file with
  veraPDF: `verapdf --format text -v --flavour 3b invoice_pdf.pdf` prints `PASS` (`brew install
  verapdf`; with Docker, see [../reference/conformance.md](../reference/conformance.md)).
- An application that would rather send a plain PDF than none rescues the claim:

  ```ruby
  begin
    InvoicePdf.new(**attributes).to_pdf
  rescue Stationery::ConformanceError => e
    Rails.logger.warn(e.message)
    InvoicePdf.new(**attributes).to_pdf(conformance: nil)
  end
  ```

- **An e-invoice** (Factur-X, ZUGFeRD, XRechnung) is the same document with
  `factur_x(profile: :en16931) { cross_industry_invoice_xml }`: it claims PDF/A-3b itself and embeds
  the XML the application writes (`stationery examples e_invoice --source` builds one).
- **User data in markup.** The party lines are a plain String, so "Müller & Söhne" prints as it is.
  With `markup: true` (for `<b>` and friends) escape it first: `ERB::Util.html_escape(name)`.
- Check it: `stationery render invoice_pdf.rb --png`, open the PNG, and read the warnings; in a spec,
  `have_pdf_text("Total due")` and `have_conformance(:pdf_a3b)`.
